<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.head(
      title: "For sales teams — CSuiteFinder",
      description: "$#{@seat_usd} per person per month, and every month that seat gets $#{@seat_usd} of lookup credit. Verified work emails and direct numbers for the people you need to reach.",
      nav: :teams,
      css: CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.page_css(:teams)
    ) %>
<header>
  <h1>Your team reaches the decision-maker. Not the contact form.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Give a name and a company, get a verified work email and a direct number.
    No API key, no terminal, and no meter running while you work.
  </p>
  <div class="switch">
    <a class="on" href="/teams">For sales teams</a>
    <a href="/developers">For developers</a>
  </div>
</header>

<h2 class="section-label" id="pricing">One price per person</h2>

<div class="plan">
  <div>
    <span class="price">$<%= @seat_usd %></span><span class="per">per person, per month</span>
  </div>
  <p class="gets">
    Every seat gets <strong>$<%= @seat_usd %> of lookup credit</strong> each month —
    a dollar of credit for a dollar paid, at the same prices everyone else pays.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <%= for item <- @seat_includes do %>
    <li><%= item %></li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <ul class="caveats">
    <%= for item <- @seat_caveats do %>
    <li><%= item %></li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/account#seats">Start a seat</a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/account">Try it free first</a>
  </p>
  <p class="cta-note" style="margin:10px 0 0">
    <%= @trial_usd %> of credit, no card, good for <%= @trial_months %> month.
    Run your own list through it before deciding anything.
  </p>
</div>

<h2 class="section-label">What your team actually does with it</h2>

<div class="does">
  <div class="item"><strong>Work a target account</strong><span>Give us the company domain and get the people at it, filtered to executives — names, titles and addresses.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Reach a named person</strong><span>You know who you need. We work out their address from how their company formats email.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Get a direct number</strong><span>A mobile or direct line for the same person, with the carrier and line type.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Check before you send</strong><span>Every address verified against the live mailbox, so bounces never touch your sending reputation.</span></div>
  <div class="item"><strong>Know who called</strong><span>A number you were called from, matched back to the person and their company.</span></div>
</div>

<h2 class="section-label">Seat or credit — which one</h2>

<p class="quiet" style="margin-top:-6px">
  The same data at the same per-lookup price. What differs is how you pay for it and
  how long it lasts:
</p>

<div class="table-scroll compare">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th></th><th class="seat">A seat — $<%= @seat_usd %>/month</th><th>Credit — bought up front</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <%= for row <- @comparison do %>
    <tr>
      <td><strong><%= row.question %></strong></td>
      <td class="seat"><%= row.seat %></td>
      <td><%= row.credit %></td>
    </tr>
    <% end %>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <strong>If you can write code and only want the data, buy credit instead.</strong>
  It is the same price per lookup, it never expires, and there is nothing to use up
  by the end of the month. A seat buys the app around the data — the browser, the
  saved work, someone to email — plus the credit to run it. We would rather say that
  now than sell you a seat you cancel in a month.
  <a href="/developers">See the developer plan →</a>
</div>

<h2 class="section-label">How the monthly credit works</h2>

<div class="step"><span class="step-n">1</span><div><strong>You are billed $<%= @seat_usd %> a month per seat.</strong> Through PayPal, on the same day each month, until you stop it.</div></div>
<div class="step"><span class="step-n">2</span><div><strong>Each payment tops the account up to $<%= @seat_usd %> a seat.</strong> That is what the lookups draw down — around <%= @seat_emails %> work emails, or <%= @seat_phones %> phone numbers, or any mix.</div></div>
<div class="step"><span class="step-n">3</span><div><strong>Whatever is left at the end of the month goes.</strong> The next payment starts you at $<%= @seat_usd %> again rather than $<%= @seat_usd %> plus the remainder. If you routinely finish the month with credit to spare, buy credit outright instead — it keeps.</div></div>
<div class="step"><span class="step-n">4</span><div><strong>Credit you bought is untouched by any of this.</strong> It sits in a separate balance that never expires, and it is spent only once the month's seat credit has run out.</div></div>

<p class="quiet">
  Cancel any time — the month you have paid for runs to its end. There is no annual
  lock-in, because a tool worth keeping should not need one.
</p>

<p style="margin-top:22px">
  <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/account#seats">Start a seat</a>
  <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="mailto:<%= @contact_email %>">Talk to us about a team</a>
</p>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.footer() %>

</div>
</body>
</html>
